WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.790
Film Courage: Are you a better writer when
you are going through happy times in your

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:07.620
life or when things are a little more uncertain
and that’s when you’re more creative?

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:14.009
Todd Berger, Co-Writer of THE HAPPYTIME MURDERS:
I think you are always kind of the same writer

00:00:14.009 --> 00:00:15.150
no matter what mood you’re in.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.230
But what you’re writing about changes.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:23.740
I think everything we write about says something
about something, right?

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:27.039
Whether it says something about the human
condition or the world.

00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:36.300
And if I’m in happier times, if I’m in
better moods, I’m probably writing something

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:45.180
that is more specifically positive or is celebrating
the good things about humanity.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:46.520
But if I’m in a darker place.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:51.760
If I’m sad or if I’m upset, I’m probably
writing something that is a biting satire

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.960
of the bad parts of the human condition.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:57.510
But probably the writing level is the same.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:02.840
I don’t think me being in a bad place or
something makes me a better writer.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:11.430
And I definitely don’t think artists need
to suffer in order to make good art.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:17.330
But I think what you are making art about
definitely probably changes and depending

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:21.260
on what you’re experiencing at any given
moment.

